/S /H1
/P 284 0 R
/T (PRICE)
/E (PRICE)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
476 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 284 0 R
/E (Many people understand how much they are willing to spend on a telescope, so it is often the most easiest place to start. Beginner visual telescopes can range anywhere from $100 to $1000. Intermediate telescopes are often ranging from $900 to $4,000. Smart telescopes range from $300 to $4000, and serious astrophotography set ups can range from $5,000 to $10,000 and up. If you have a pretty large price range, Size and purpose can still steer you in the correct direction.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
477 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 284 0 R
/E (There are three big things to consider when trying to figure out what kind of telescope to purchase, and they are also all connected. This  is often the easiest place to start and can aid your decision making. They are Price, Size, and Purpose \(in no particular order\).)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
478 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 284 0 R
/T (PRICE)
/E (PRICE)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
