482 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 284 0 R
/T (PURPOSE)
/E (PURPOSE)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
483 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 284 0 R
/E (The bigger in price often translates into the bigger in size of telescope. The size of the telescope often can be understood through aperture. Often times with a bigger aperature you also get a longer focal length. If you would like a bigger aperture but dont want a super long telescope, a Schmidt Cassegrain telescope may be the way to go. Along with bigger aperture, the more heavy your telescope gets which needs to be taken into consideration. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
484 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 284 0 R
/E (Now Size affects your viewing. With the smaller telescopes you will see minimal details and the bigger you get with telescopes the more details and fainter objects you can see.  This is where purpose is important; What are you wanting to see? Would you like to see mainly planets, stars, and fast moving objects, or would you like to see galaxies, star clusters, nebulas, and more dim objects? Are you interested in astrophotography and would you be willing to post process your own images?)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
