781 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 290 0 R
/E (To step up your set up, you may be interested in an equatorial mount. Unlike the previous options that move up and down, left and right \(ALT/AZ\), an equatorial moves in an arc motion using right ascension and declination to smoothly follow objects in the sky. These mounts are usually more heavier and bulky than a basic mount, but offer more simplicity to follow objects. These mounts can also hold heavier telescopes as they have counterweights to balance your whole rig. Equatorial mounts also can come computerized which are amazing for astrophotography!)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
782 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 290 0 R
/E (There are many types of computerized go to mounts that all work in a similar fashion. Each have a hand controller to electronically move the telescope where you want it to point. To use the GO TO feature you need to insert the date, time, and location and do a three star alignment so that the scope knows where it is and what the sky looks like. The three star alignment is usually where folks get stuck on as you have to locate and center three bright stars quite a distance apart, in the telescope. Once done successfully, the telescope will give a list of objects for that night that you can choose from and will automatically find and track the object you choose! Each of these three mounts work the same, the main difference is that the mounts get progressively more heavy duty for bigger telescopes. The Evolution and CPC mount have the capability of being moved manually and can hold more weight, great for adding on cameras!)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
